# [DHCP Server Deployment on Homelab AD Domain Controller]
Date: [08/2026]
Status: [completed]

---

## Overview
set up the DHCP Server role on the homelab Windows Server 2025 DC to replace default DHCP with a properly scoped, domain-aware setup. clients get IP leases from the DC, use the DC for DNS 
(required for AD name resolution, GPO, and domain join to function correctly), and the DC forwards any non-domain DNS queries to the homelab's Pi-hole so ad-blocking applies network-wide 
without breaking AD resolution.

---

## Environment
- Device: [Windows Server 2025 DC]
- OS: [Windows Server 2025]
- Domain: [homelab.local]
- Subnet: [private]
- DC IP: [private]
- Pi-hole IP: [private]
- Tooling: [PowerShell, DHCP Server role, DNS Manager]

---

## Steps
1. [installed the DHCP Server role and management tools: Install-WindowsFeature -Name DHCP -IncludeManagementTools]
2. [authorized the DHCP server in Active Directory with Add-DhcpServerInDC — hit a PermissionDenied error on the first attempt because the logged-in account wasn't in Enterprise Admins; 
   added the account to that group and re-ran the command successfully]
3. [created a scope for the subnet: Add-DhcpServerv4Scope, /24, named Homelab-LAN]
4. [set scope options with Set-DhcpServerv4OptionValue so clients get the DC's IP as DNS server, keeping AD name resolution working for domain-joined clients]
5. [added a DNS forwarder on the DC pointing to the Pi-hole via DNS Manager, so queries the DC isn't authoritative for get forwarded to Pi-hole for ad-blocking, while homelab.local records still 
   resolve locally]
6. [excluded the low end of the subnet with Add-DhcpServerv4ExclusionRange to protect the DC's static IP and other statically assigned devices from being handed out by DHCP]
7. [ran Get-DhcpServerv4Lease on the DC to check for issued leases — came back empty, which led to troubleshooting why the scope wasn't actually being used]
8. [traced the issue to VMware Workstation's own virtual network (VMnet8, matching the lab subnet) running its own built-in DHCP service and answering client requests instead of the DC — 
   confirmed via the Virtual Network Editor, where VMnet8 showed DHCP: Enabled]
9. [disabled VMware's local DHCP service on VMnet8 so the DC's DHCP scope would be the only one answering on that subnet]
10. [client fell back to an APIPA address (169.254.x.x) after the change, meaning the DHCP negotiation between the Windows 11 client and the DC isn't completing yet — confirmed the DC itself is 
    healthy (DHCP authorized, DHCPServer service running, scope active) so the remaining issue is isolated to the client-to-DC network path, not the DHCP configuration itself]

---

## Issues Encountered
- initial DHCP authorization failed with a PermissionDenied error — resolved by adding the account to Enterprise Admins
- ad-blocking initially appeared to work when tested, but the Pi-hole query log showed the source IP as the physical workstation, not the VM — turned out the browser test was run on the host 
  machine through the nested remote session, not inside the VM's guest OS, so it wasn't actually testing the DHCP/DNS forwarder chain at all
- discovered VMware Workstation's own DHCP service on VMnet8 was answering client leases instead of the DC, despite the DC's scope being properly configured and active — disabled it in the 
  Virtual Network Editor
- after disabling VMware's DHCP, the Windows 11 client isn't yet completing a DHCP lease with the DC (falls back to APIPA) — DC-side config confirmed healthy, root cause still being isolated 
  to the client/network path

---

## Result
DHCP role installed, authorized in AD, and scope active on the DC with correct options and an exclusion range in place. DNS forwarder to Pi-hole configured. root cause of the DHCP conflict 
(VMware's competing DHCP service) identified and resolved. client-to-DC lease negotiation not fully verified end to end yet — considering this stage complete for now and will revisit the 
remaining connectivity issue separately.

---

## Notes
good reminder that "it looks like it's working" isn't the same as verifying the actual path — the ad-blocking test passing on the wrong machine masked the fact that nothing was really being 
tested yet. also a good example of environment quirks that don't show up in documentation: VMware Workstation runs its own DHCP service by default on NAT networks, which will silently compete 
with anything set up inside the guest OS if it's not turned off first.
